/**
 * =============================================================================
 * KS-732 — disabling MFA must cost a proof of possession
 * =============================================================================
 * The defect: `POST /api/auth/mfa/disable` REQUIRED `password` in its schema and
 * never read it. Measured: `password` appears exactly once in routes/mfa.ts (the
 * zod schema), and `bcrypt|compare(|verifyPassword` matched 0 times in that file
 * against 6 in routes/auth.ts — the control that makes the zero a real negative
 * rather than a grep that could not match. The TOTP branch was guarded on
 * `data.totpCode` being PRESENT, so omitting it skipped verification entirely.
 * Any bearer disabled MFA with {"password":"anything"}.
 *
 * WHY NOT SIMPLY "VERIFY THE PASSWORD"
 * ------------------------------------
 * The ticket recommended that first. It is unsafe: getOrCreateWalletUser()
 * builds its User with NO passwordHash (0 occurrences in routes/wallet.ts
 * against 10 in routes/auth.ts) and `password_hash` is NULLABLE since migration
 * 001. Requiring a password makes this route UNSATISFIABLE for a wallet account
 * — MFA could be enabled and never removed. So the rule is the strongest proof
 * the account can actually produce, never nothing.
 *
 * `backupCode` is a SEPARATE field and had to be. `totpCode` is
 * `.length(6).regex(/^\d+$/)` and generateBackupCodes() emits 8-char base32, so
 * a backup code cannot arrive through it; a branch reading one from `totpCode`
 * would be dead code that this file would have reported as covered.
 *
 * WHAT IS STUBBED, AND WHY EACH STUB CAN STILL FAIL
 * ------------------------------------------------
 * `verifyPassword`, `verifyTotpCode` and `verifyBackupCode` are state-driven
 * stubs, not passthroughs — each returns false unless the test says otherwise,
 * so a handler that skipped a check would still be caught. `../db` is stubbed so
 * the REAL userRepo runs against it and the disable WRITE is observed rather
 * than inferred. `authenticate` is a gating stand-in.
 *
 * CELL 1 IS THE COMPATIBILITY INVARIANT: a password account sending only a
 * correct password must still succeed. If that ever reds, this stopped being a
 * security fix and became a contract change.
 * CELL 7 IS LOAD-BEARING: it asserts the success path actually WRITES. Without
 * it every "succeeds" cell here is satisfied by a 200 that does nothing — the
 * KS-720 defect wearing this ticket's clothes.
 * =============================================================================
 */

import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll, beforeEach, vi } from 'vitest';
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import type { Server } from 'node:http';
import type { AddressInfo } from 'node:net';
import express from 'express';

const USER_ID = '3c9a1f77-5b22-4e88-9a13-6d4e2f8b0c55';

